<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Satire of the Trades</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Instruction of Kheti, Satire des Métiers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Satire of the Trades, also called the Instruction of Kheti, is a didactic work of ancient Egyptian literature written during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>. In it a scribe from Sile named Kheti tells his son Pepi that the scribe's career is greater than any other, describing in exaggerated terms the toil of smiths, masons, potters and other workers. Copied widely as a schoolboy text, it was among the most copied texts in Theban scribal schools in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twentieth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twentieth Dynasty</a> (1189–1077 BCE); the whole text survives on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> Sallier II in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Egyptologists disagree on whether it is satire: Miriam Lichtheim held that it is, while Chris Rollston reads it as scribal propaganda.</p></div>
